  "outro": [
   "What's *your* blood type?",
   "Send this to your family group chat."
  ],
  "grid_title": "BLOOD TYPES IN THE US",
  "why": "Blood type is set by two markers (antigens), A and B, on the red cells, plus the Rh factor (RhD): present is positive, absent is negative. Group A has anti-B antibodies in the plasma, group B has anti-A, group O has both and AB has neither; the wrong ABO group can be life-threatening. US share (American Red Cross): O+ 37-38% (most common), A+ about 1 in 3, B+ about 9%, O- 7%, A- 1 in 16, AB+ under 4%, B- under 2%, AB- under 1% (rarest). O negative is the universal red cell donor; AB positive is the universal recipient; Rh-negative patients get Rh-negative red cells. For plasma the rules are reversed: AB plasma can go to any type.",
